      <section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Define the jobs you actually want</h2><p>An emergency AC repair, a complete heating system replacement and a commercial rooftop installation are not interchangeable opportunities. Before buying traffic, identify the jobs your company wants more of and the territory your team can serve reliably.</p><p>Prioritize services using contribution margin, technician capacity, seasonality, average sales cycle and job value. A lower-volume replacement campaign might be more useful than a high-volume campaign generating minor service inquiries, depending on your business model.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Capture demand when customers need a contractor</h2><p>Google Search can reach people actively looking for AC repair, heating repair, HVAC installation or a replacement estimate. The exact terms and local competition vary by service area. Match the advertisement to the service and the page a customer sees after clicking.</p><p>SEO should support the same service priorities with accurate service pages, useful location information, verified business details and helpful answers to common customer questions. Organic visibility develops differently from paid campaigns, so treat them as complementary channels.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Use seasonal campaigns without relying entirely on emergencies</h2><p>Cooling and heating demand can change with weather, equipment age, local climate and seasonal maintenance cycles. Emergency campaigns may generate urgent inquiries, while maintenance and replacement offers help introduce the company before a system fails.</p><p>Plan campaigns around local conditions and actual crew capacity. Avoid promising immediate availability, fixed installation prices or guaranteed service response times unless the business can consistently deliver them.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Build the right landing page for each type of job</h2><p>A repair landing page should make it easy to call or request service, confirm geographic coverage and describe the response process. A replacement page can explain the estimate process, relevant system considerations and what information the contractor needs.</p><p>Commercial HVAC campaigns may require separate pages describing building type, equipment scope, service capabilities and consultation steps. Routing every search to a generic homepage can obscure the offer and make qualification harder.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Qualify inquiries without making customers complete an inspection</h2><p>Start with the information useful for routing: location, service needed, property type, preferred contact method and approximate timing. For replacements, ask whether the inquiry concerns an existing system, a planned project or an urgent failure.</p><p>Do not require homeowners to provide technical diagnoses. Questions about refrigerant, electrical components and system safety belong with trained professionals, not a lead form. Keep emergency and accessibility considerations in mind when designing the intake process.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Measure what happens after the lead arrives</h2><p>Track advertising source, inquiry type, valid contact details, service-area fit, booked appointments, completed estimates and won jobs. Call recordings or detailed tracking should only be used with appropriate notice and compliance.</p><p>A high inquiry count does not necessarily mean profitable growth. Review cost per qualified inquiry, booking rate, estimate attendance, close rate and job margin. Use those outcomes to improve campaigns, staffing and follow-up instead of optimizing for form submissions alone.</p></section>

      <section class="lf-article-section">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <h3>What is a qualified HVAC lead?</h3><p>An inquiry from someone in the contractor&#x27;s service area with a genuine heating or cooling need, suitable job type, usable contact details and a realistic next step.</p>
<h3>Should repair and replacement campaigns be separate?</h3><p>Usually, yes. Their urgency, landing-page content, qualification questions and economics differ significantly.</p>
      </section>

